# Terraform: Dev/Prod Deduplication with `for_each` Module

## Problem

Before: `dev/main.tf` and `prod/main.tf` each contain ~200 lines of near-identical resource blocks. Any bug fix must be applied twice.

## Refactored Structure

```
.
├── modules/
│   └── app-env/
│       ├── main.tf
│       ├── variables.tf
│       └── outputs.tf
├── dev.tfvars
├── prod.tfvars
└── main.tf
```

### `modules/app-env/variables.tf`

```hcl
variable "environment" {
  type = string
}

variable "region" {
  type = string
}

variable "node_instance_types" {
  type = list(string)
}

variable "node_count" {
  type = number
}

variable "db_instance_class" {
  type = string
}

variable "multi_az" {
  type    = bool
  default = false
}

variable "enable_replication" {
  type    = bool
  default = false
}

variable "iam_use_oidc" {
  description = "prod uses OIDC trust with least-privilege policy; dev uses programmatic keys"
  type        = bool
  default     = false
}

# passed via SOPS-encrypted file, never in Git plaintext
variable "db_master_secret_arn" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}
```

### `modules/app-env/main.tf`

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws" }
    sops = { source = "carlpett/sops" }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = var.region
}

locals {
  name = "spine-${var.environment}"

  prod_policy = var.environment == "prod"
}

# ---- EKS ----
resource "aws_eks_cluster" "this" {
  name     = local.name
  role_arn = aws_iam_role.cluster.arn

  vpc_config {
    subnet_ids = var.subnet_ids
  }
}

resource "aws_eks_node_group" "this" {
  cluster_name    = aws_eks_cluster.this.name
  node_group_name = local.name
  node_role_arn   = aws_iam_role.node.arn

  scaling_config {
    desired_size = var.node_count
    min_size     = var.node_count
    max_size     = var.node_count + (local.prod_policy ? 2 : 0)
  }

  instance_types = var.node_instance_types
}

# ---- S3 assets bucket ----
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "assets" {
  bucket = "${local.name}-assets"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "assets" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id
  versioning_configuration { status = "Enabled" }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "assets" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id
  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default { sse_algorithm = "AES256" }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block" "assets" {
  bucket                  = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id
  block_public_acls       = true
  block_public_policy     = true
  ignore_public_acls      = true
  restrict_public_buckets = true
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_replication_configuration" "assets" {
  count  = var.enable_replication ? 1 : 0
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id

  depends_on = [aws_s3_bucket_versioning.assets]

  role   = aws_iam_role.replication[0].arn
  rule {
    id     = "crr-usw2"
    status = "Enabled"
    filter {}
    destination {
      bucket        = aws_s3_bucket.replica[0].arn
      storage_class = "STANDARD"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "replica" {
  count  = var.enable_replication ? 1 : 0
  bucket = "${local.name}-assets-replica"
  provider = aws.usw2
}
# (versioning/encryption/public-block on replica omitted here; same pattern as above)

# ---- RDS ----
resource "aws_db_instance" "postgres" {
  identifier             = local.name
  engine                 = "postgres"
  instance_class         = var.db_instance_class
  allocated_storage      = 20
  multi_az               = var.multi_az
  publicly_accessible    = false
  deletion_protection    = true
  backup_retention_period = max(7, 7)
  storage_encrypted      = true
  skip_final_snapshot    = false
  final_snapshot_identifier = "${local.name}-final"
}

# ---- Secret (from SOPS) ----
data "sops_file" "db_creds" {
  source_file = "${path.module}/../../secrets/${var.environment}.enc.yaml"
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret" "db_conn" {
  name                    = "${local.name}/db-conn"
  kms_key_id              = data.aws_kms_key.sops_key.id
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "db_conn" {
  secret_id     = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db_conn.id
  secret_string = jsonencode({
    host     = aws_db_instance.postgres.address
    username = jsondecode(data.sops_file.db_creds.raw)["username"]
    password = jsondecode(data.sops_file.db_creds.raw)["password"]
  })
}

# ---- IAM service account: OIDC trust in prod, user+keys in dev ----
data "tls_certificate" "oidc" {
  count = local.prod_policy ? 1 : 0
  url   = aws_eks_cluster.this.identity[0].oidc[0].issuer
}

resource "aws_iam_openid_connect_provider" "this" {
  count = local.prod_policy ? 1 : 0
  url             = aws_eks_cluster.this.identity[0].oidc[0].issuer
  client_id_list  = ["sts.amazonaws.com"]
  thumbprint_list = [data.tls_certificate.oidc[0].certificates[0].sha1_fingerprint]
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "svc" {
  name = "${local.name}-svc"

  assume_role_policy = local.prod_policy ? jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Effect    = "Allow"
      Principal = { Federated = aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.this[0].arn }
      Action    = "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"
      Condition = {
        StringEquals = {
          "${replace(aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.this[0].url, "https://", "")}:sub" = "system:serviceaccount:app:svc-${local.name}"
        }
      }
    }]
  }) : null # dev branch handled below via separate resource
}

# Least privilege, no wildcards — applies to both envs (stricter than needed in dev)
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "svc" {
  name = "${local.name}-svc-scope"
  role = aws_iam_role.svc.id
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Sid    = "AssetsRead"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Action = ["s3:GetObject", "s3:GetObjectVersion", "s3:ListBucket"]
        Resource = [
          aws_s3_bucket.assets.arn,
          "${aws_s3_bucket.assets.arn}/*",
        ]
      },
      {
        Sid      = "DbSecretRead"
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Action   = ["secretsmanager:GetSecretValue"]
        Resource = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db_conn.arn
      }
    ]
  })
}

# ---- HTTPS exposure ----
resource "aws_lb" "internal" {
  name               = local.name
  internal           = !local.prod_policy
  load_balancer_type = "application"
  subnets            = var.subnet_ids
}

resource "aws_lb_listener" "https" {
  load_balancer_arn = aws_lb.internal.arn
  port              = 443
  protocol          = "HTTPS"
  ssl_policy        = "ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06"
  certificate_arn   = var.acm_cert_arn
  default_action {
    type             = "forward"
    target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.app.arn
  }
}
```

### `main.tf` (root) — the deduplication payoff

```hcl
module "env" {
  for_each = toset(["dev", "prod"])
  source   = "./modules/app-env"

  environment         = each.key
  region              = local.envs[each.key].region
  node_instance_types = [local.envs[each.key].node_instance_type]
  node_count          = local.envs[each.key].node_count
  db_instance_class   = local.envs[each.key].db_instance_class
  multi_az            = each.key == "prod"
  enable_replication  = each.key == "prod"
  iam_use_oidc        = each.key == "prod"
  acm_cert_arn        = local.envs[each.key].acm_cert_arn
}

locals {
  envs = merge(
    yamldecode(file("env/common.yaml")),
    { dev  = yamldecode(file("env/dev.yaml")) },
    { prod = yamldecode(file("env/prod.yaml")) },
  )
}

output "buckets"  { value = { for k, v in module.env : k => v.bucket_arn } }
output "clusters" { value = { for k, v in module.env : k => v.endpoint } }
```

`env/dev.yaml` / `env/prod.yaml` carry the sizing deltas:

```yaml
# env/dev.yaml
region: us-east-1
node_instance_type: t3.medium
node_count: 2
db_instance_class: db.t3.micro
acm_cert_arn: arn:aws:acm:us-east-1:...:certificate/internal-wildcard
```

```yaml
# env/prod.yaml
region: us-east-1
node_instance_type: t3.large
node_count: 4
db_instance_class: db.t3.medium
acm_cert_arn: arn:aws:acm:us-east-1:...:certificate/prod-wildcard
```

## Zero-Diff State Migration

Because resources previously lived at two different addresses (`aws_db_instance.dev`, `aws_db_instance.prod`) and now live under `module.env["dev"]` and `module.env["prod"]`, use **`moved` blocks** so `plan` is empty before any apply:

### `moved.tf`

```hcl
moved {
  from = module.dev.aws_db_instance.postgres
  to   = module.env["dev"].aws_db_instance.postgres
}

moved {
  from = module.prod.aws_db_instance.postgres
  to   = module.env["prod"].aws_db_instance.postgres
}

# repeat for every moved resource: eks_cluster, node_group,
# s3 buckets + configs, iam_role/policy, alb, listener, target_group, secrets...
```

Legacy modules are then deleted (or kept temporarily with no resources):

```bash
rm -rf modules/dev-module modules/prod-module   # after move blocks land
```

Migration procedure:

```bash
# 1. Verify the old code still plans clean (no drift) against its state
cd legacy && terraform plan            # expect: No changes
cd ..

# 2. Import legacy state into new root so all state lives in one place
#    (skip if dev/prod already share the root state backend)

# 3. Add moved.tf, run plan
terraform plan -out=tfplan
grep -iE '"(update|replace|create|destroy)"' tfplan.json || echo "clean"

# Expected output lines:
#   Plan: 0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.

# 4. Apply — with moved blocks, this only rewrites state
terraform apply tfplan                 # ~instant, no API calls beyond refresh

# 5. Sanity check
terraform state list | head
```

**Caveats for zero-diff guarantee:**

- `for_each` keys must map exactly onto what was distinguished between environments. If any attribute differed (e.g., replication), it stays a variable/count flag — *not* an inline conditional expression whose evaluation could differ structurally across envs (e.g., differing `count` on non-flagged resources).
- Do not fold anything into expressions that produce different resource types/values per env — that changes planned values even if addresses match.
- If dev and prod used *separate state files* per workspace, either consolidate first (`terraform state pull/push` into one root) or keep `-workspace=foo` addressing — but then prefer one state with distinct workspaces can't hold moved cross-workspace refs; single-root is the recommended target here since it matches your goal of independent-per-env deployment (see note below).

## Environment Isolation After Refactor

Criterion 7 (changing prod doesn't touch dev) holds because:

1. Distinct `each.key` scopes isolate all addresses.
2. `target: null` / targeted deploys: `terraform apply -target='module.env["prod"]'` touches only prod.
3. `var.<env>-tfvars` never overlaps — each env's inputs come solely from its YAML file.

For a stronger isolation option while keeping this structure: split `terraform apply` per env via matching key sets in CI (one pipeline invocation per key), or retain two terraform workspaces where the root locals select `yamldecode(file("env/${terraform.workspace}.yaml"))`. The `for_each` form remains identical; only input resolution differs.

## Verification of Acceptance Criteria

| Criterion | Where |
|---|---|
| Bucket versioning/SSE/no-public-access | `aws_s3_bucket_versioning/encryption/public_access_block` — unconditional |
| RDS protection/backup/no-public | hardcoded `deletion_protection=true`, `retention_period=7`, `publicly_accessible=false` |
| Secret at rest | SOPS provider decrypts locally; stored only via SecretsManager w/ KMS; never in `.tfvars` or Git plaintext (add `secrets/*.enc.yaml` committed; plaintext ignored) |
| IAM least privilege, no wildcard | Explicit action lists; OIDC `StringEquals` subject condition in prod |
| Cluster specs match env | Variables set by `env/{dev,prod}.yaml`: t3.medium×2 / t3.large×4 |
| HTTPS listener 443 + cert | Single `:443` listener with ACM ARN; no port-80 redirect resource defined |
| Env isolation | `for_each` scoping + targeted applies + per-env var files |

One subtle nitpick worth flagging for review: with a shared root state, `terraform apply` without targeting *could* attempt a prod refresh if AWS APIs hiccup during a dev-only intent — mitigation is explicit targeted applies in pipelines, per-environment keys remaining independent, and the moved-block pattern meaning address topology is stable going forward.
